          %h2#MapsBetweenTopologicalSpaces Motivation: maps between topological spaces
          %p
            Classes of maps between
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %p
            To pick just one example: there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            that goes by the name
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z},2)
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
            and is called an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+space"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathcal{B}U(1)]
            of continuous maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
            characterize the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                they correspond to equivalence classes of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                equipped with a map to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                such that over a contractible open subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
                the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                looks like the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                with the circle,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi U
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi U
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P|_U \simeq U \times S^1
                (and such that the identification respects the action of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                on the circle).
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    Thus, one says that
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi ℬ
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
                    is the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                    for circle
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                    = succeed "circle" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ℬ
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ≃
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathcal{B}U(1)] \simeq \{
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo ∼
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \}/{\sim}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represents
                    the assignment of circle bundles to spaces – to some extent the topic of sheaves, and cohomology is all about the notion of such
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representability
                %li
                  %p
                    A famous such circle bundle you may have seen is the
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
                    to the familiar 2-dimensional sphere
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                          %mo ⊂
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset S^2
                    of the 2-sphere the 3-sphere looks like the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                    space
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi U
                            %mo ×
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times S^1
                %li
                  %p
                    This particular circle bundle corresponds to a very special class of maps
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo →
                            %mi ℬ
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathcal{B}U(1)
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [S^2, \mathcal{B}U(1)]
                    happens to have the structure of the abelian group
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℤ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                    of integers under addition, and the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
                    corresponds to the generating element
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                    of this group;
                %li
                  %p
                    generally, this abelian group formed by
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathcal{B}U(1)]
                    is famous under another name: it is called the second integral
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                    group of
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi H
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^2(X, \mathbb{Z})
            %li
              %p
                There is a similar such cohomology group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{n+1}(X,\mathbb{Z})
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                for every natural number
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "st" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                %em integral cohomology group
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                – and all these groups are naturally realized in terms of homotopy classes of maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                into some topological space – called the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}, n+1)
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℬ
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}^{n} U(1)
                – and again each element in these groups can be identified with a certain geometric structure living over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "-bundle" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
                = succeed "circle" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mi n
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathcal{B}^{n} U(1)] = \{
                = succeed "-bundles" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \}/{\sim}
          %p So these cohomology groups encode a lot of interesting information about topological spaces; but there is some useful information about a space, which is not encoded in maps from it to another topological space:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in particular, if the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                in question is not just a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                but carries extra
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                %em smooth
                circle bundles over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                which are equipped with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∇
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo :
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : x \to y
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                an identification between the two circles over its endpoints;
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi x
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ↦
                                %mo ∇
                            %mtd
                              %msubsup
                                %mi S
                                %mi x
                                %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mi γ
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∇
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi γ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi y
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ↦
                                %mo ∇
                            %mtd
                              %msubsup
                                %mi S
                                %mi y
                                %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      x &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\nabla}{\mapsto} &amp; S^1_x
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow^\gamma
                      \&amp;&amp;&amp;
                      \\downarrow^{\nabla(\gamma)}
                      \\\
                      y &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\nabla}{\mapsto} &amp; S^1_y
                      }
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    this is the point where these considerations become of interest for
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundles"} bundles
                    with
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} n-bundles
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
                    = succeed "s," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
                    such as the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
                    for more on this.
                %li
                  %p
                    you might guess that there is a smooth manifold classifying smooth circle bundles with connection – but there is not, not unless one stretches the meaning of
                    %em manifold
                    and of
                    %em space
                    in general a bit
                %li
                  %p so maybe we just need a slightly more flexible notion of what “space” should mean.
            %li
              %p
                the generalization of the notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space
                which does accomplish this: the notion of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              %ul
                %li
                  (there is a very general abstract nonsense way to understand
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
                  as generalized spaces in the context of a very general abstract
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
                  between the notions of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
          %h2#TheBasicIdeaOfSheaves The basic idea of sheaves
          %p
            To understand what a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            is, recall which aspect of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            was relevant in the above examples
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi n
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}^{n} U(1)
            %em map
            other spaces
            %em into
            them:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi n
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto [X,\mathcal{B}^{n} U(1)] .
          %p
            This is a general strategy that one can adopt: suppose I dream up a space but don’t tell you which one it is, but I give you hints: for each other space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            that you can dream up, I tell you how you can
            %em probe
            my space by mapping your space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            into it.
          %ul
            %li
              %dl
                %dt
                  Let’s now call the space which I dreamed up
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  that you come up with, I do some secret computation and then present you with the result: I hand you a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  can be mapped into
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %dd
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U) = \{
                    ways to map
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                    into some hypothetical
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \}
                %dd
                  %p
                      =: {probes of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    by
                    = succeed "}." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi U
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              %p
                Will you be able, in general, to guess my space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                from this information
                = succeed "?" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is like, you need to know something about how different ways of probing my space relate to each other.
            %li
              %p
                If you give me a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{f}{\to} V
                from one test space to another, then for any element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \stackrel{p}{\to} X
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{f}{\to} V \stackrel{p}{\to} X
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to V
                induces a transformation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ←
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U) \leftarrow X(V)
            %li
              %p
                Well, fine: I’ll do it. If you give me a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{f}{\to} V
                from one test space to another, I’ll give you a map of sets, denoted
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U) \stackrel{X(f)}{\leftarrow} X(V)
                = succeed "-probes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                turn into
                = succeed "-probes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            Do you need still more information to guess my space
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            and its implication on
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
          %p
            First, there is a bit more to the game than mentioned so far: you need to be sure that even if I won’t reveal my space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to you directly, the little information about it which I do provide, that rule
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it must be true that when you hand me the identity map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi U
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_U : U \to U
                on a test space, that then I return you the identity map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_{X(U)} : X(U) \to X(U)
                on the set of probes of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              %p
                also, it must be true that when you first hand me two consecutive maps of test spaces,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : U \to V
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi V
                        %mo →
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : V \to W
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f : U \to W
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(g \circ f) : X(W) \to X(U)
                to the latter is the result of composing my two replies about the former:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(g \circ f) = X(W) \stackrel{X(g)}{\to} X(V) \stackrel{X(f)}{\to} X(U)
          %p
            Such an assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            of sets to spaces which satisfies the above two properties is what is called a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                in question here is the collection of test
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : U \to V
                between them, called
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                there is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity
                map
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Id
                          %mi U
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_U : U \to U
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                = succeed "topological" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Top
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top = \{
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \}
            %li
              %p
                the other
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                in the game, namely the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                called
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                whose
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                = succeed "sets" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set = \{
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \}
            %li
              %p
                the probe-game which we are playing can hence be understood as being an assignment of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                to
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                and of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                to
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                which satisfies the above consistency conditions: this is called a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                and it is denoted
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi Top
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-) : Top^{op} \to Set
          %p
            We say
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            instead of just
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which you should do: it must be true that you can piece together the information which I give for small test spaces to deduce information about probes by bigger test spaces.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                More precisely, if I tell you the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                of probes of my secret space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by the test space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                into two pieces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
                sitting inside
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                by inclusion maps
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mi i
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i : V_i \hookrightarrow U
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \cap V_2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                can be entirely and exactly reconstructed from taking probes by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V_1)
                and by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V_2)
                and see if they match over the overlap
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \cap V_2
                of the two small probes:
            %li
              %p
                in symbols, the collection of pairs in
                = succeed "matching" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \} \hookrightarrow X(V_1) \times X(V_2)
                whose elements coincide when restricted to the overlap
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %munderover
                        %mphantom
                          %mi AAA
                        %munder
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %mi restrict
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi second
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi element
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %mi restrict
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi first
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi element
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      X(V_1) \times X(V_2)
                      \\underoverset
                      {\underset{restrict\;second\;element}{\longrightarrow}}
                      {\overset{restrict\;first\;element}{\longrightarrow}}
                      {\phantom{AAA}}
                      X(V_1 \cap V_2)
              %p
                should be exactly all the possible maps in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                = succeed "matching" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2 \} \simeq X(U)
            %li
              %p
                one says that the set
                = succeed "matching" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2 \}
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
                of the two
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+morphisms"} parallel morphisms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %munderover
                        %mphantom
                          %mi AAA
                        %munder
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %mi restrict
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi second
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi element
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %mi restrict
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi first
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi element
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      X(V_1) \times X(V_2)
                      \\underoverset
                      {\underset{restrict\;second\;element}{\longrightarrow}}
                      {\overset{restrict\;first\;element}{\longrightarrow}}
                      {\phantom{AAA}}
                      X(V_1 \cap V_2)
              %p
                the optimal solution to mapping into
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V_1) \times X(V_2)
                such that these two maps become equal;
            %li
              %p
                this condition is called a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                condition: one thinks of the sheaf of
                %em descending
                from the cover to the base along the map
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊔
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ⊔
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mn 2
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_1 \sqcup V_2) \stackrel{p_1 \sqcup p_2}{\to} U
          %p
            This now is a sensible game to play: I don’t tell you directly which space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            I am thinking of, but I do give you all the information
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            about its probes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
            by test spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-)
                is functorial and hence a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-)
                satisfies
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                and is therefore a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
          %h2#sheaves_more_general_than_spaces Sheaves more general than spaces
          %p
            So far we have been talking about using topological spaces as probe spaces, hence about presheaves on the category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of test spaces as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            and such that there is an agreement about how some of its
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "ed" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
            above. Such an agreement of what counts as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            or a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            equipped with such an information is called a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          %p Here are some important examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A popular choice is to fix one particular topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                and probe all generalized spaces using only open subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset Z
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi Op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = Op(Z)
                and the choice of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
                is the obvious one.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} Sheaves
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(Z)
                are generalized spaces
                %em over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %li
              %p
                Another relevant choice is to use instead of all topological test spaces in the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                all smooth manifolds as test spaces, in the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S =
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            %li
              %p
                Or the combination of these: the test spaces in the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(Z)
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                a smooth manifold.
          %p
            We had motivated this language of sheaves as a reformulation of the familar notion of spaces in a somewhat more indirect form: a rule for how to probe a space. But now something strange happens: it turns out that there are collections of probe information
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-) : S^{op} \to Set
            which do obey the rules, i.e. do form a sheaf, but for which there is nevertheless
            %em no
            test space in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            that it describes probes into. And in fact, this is the generic case.
          %ul
            %li
              %p On the other hand, maybe this just means that our notion of “ordinary space” was a bit too restrictive: after all, we noticed that all that really matters usually are the ways we can probe a given space by other spaces; and that the information about such probes is useful if only it obeys the rules of being a sheaf.
            %li
              %p
                So we have every justification then to regard sheaves in general as perfectly valid
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em generalized spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
                of probes.
          %p Examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The notion of “generalized” here depends on the perspective of our probes. For the probe category of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi Op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = Op(Z)
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                a topological space, most sheaves are “generalized spaces” in that they do not describe maps into a space in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(Z)
                can be shown to correspond to maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                to an ordinary topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                which is equipped with a continuous map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : E \to Z
                down to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is locally a homeomorphism. Such
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                are called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+space"} étale spaces
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %li
              %p
                A sheaf on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S =
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
          %h2#maps_between_generalized_spaces Maps between generalized spaces
          %p
            Since we are thinking of sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X : S^{op} \to Set
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_Y : S^{op} \to Set
            as characterizing ‘generalized spaces’
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X \to F_Y
            that corresponds to a sensible notion of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            between generalized spaces.
          %p
            That’s easy to figure out: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            is supposed to be a map between generalized spaces, whatever these are, then in particular it turns every probe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which we are thinking of as a probe map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : U \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ p \in Y(U)
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ p : U \stackrel{p}{\to} X \stackrel{f}{\to} Y
            that simply maps the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            further to an image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            using the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                nota bene: for the time being there is no space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                such that thinking of probes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X(U)
                as maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : U \to X
                of some sort would literally make sense. However, once we impose some consistency conditions on the probe sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                precisely this will make sense. And to motivate these consistency conditions in the first place, it is best to already think of probes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X(U)
                as being maps
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : U \to X
            %li
              %p
                so in terms of their collections (sheaves) of probes a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                of generalized spaces should be given for each probe space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                by a map of sets of probes
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi U
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_U : X(U) \to Y(U)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi U
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_U : (U \stackrel{p}{\to} X) \mapsto (U \stackrel{p}{\to} X \stackrel{f}{\to} Y)
            %li
              %p
                but we can also pre-compose: given a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : U \to V
                of test spaces, we also have the probe transformation map from above,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\phi) : X(V) \to X(U)
                which is to be thought of as acting by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi ϕ
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\phi) : (V \stackrel{p}{\to} X) \mapsto (U \stackrel{\phi}{\to} V \stackrel{p}{\to} X)
            %li
              %p
                and we can combine both pre- and postcomposition and send
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi ϕ
                      %mi V
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V \stackrel{p}{\to} X) \mapsto (U \stackrel{\phi}{\to} V \stackrel{p}{\to} X \stackrel{f}{\to} Y)
            %li
              %p
                in this notation it is manifestly clear that it must not matter whether we first pre- and then post-compose, or the other way around. But recall that so far writing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : V \to X
                for a probe
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X(V)
                is just notation. So we need to enforce the obvious here on our formalism explicitly, lest the interpretation of generalized spaces breaks down.
            %li
              %p
                namely this means that the map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mi V
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo *
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V) \stackrel{f_V}{\to} Y(V) \stackrel{\phi^*}{\to} Y(U)
                which first turns a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                into a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                and then turns the result into a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo *
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mi U
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(V) \stackrel{\phi^*}{\to} X(U) \stackrel{f_U}{\to} Y(U)
                which first turns a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                into a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and then turns the result into a
                = succeed "-probe" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %li
              %p Such an equality between two different composite maps is conveniently depicted as a square
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi U
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi f
                                    %mi U
                            %mtd
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi U
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                                %mrow
                                  %mi X
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi ϕ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                                %mrow
                                  %mi Y
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi ϕ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi V
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi f
                                    %mi V
                            %mtd
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi V
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      X(U)
                      \&amp;\stackrel{f_U}{\to}&amp;
                      Y(U)
                      \\\
                      \\uparrow^{X(\phi)}
                      \&amp;&amp;
                      \\uparrow^{Y(\phi)}
                      \\\
                      X(V)
                      \&amp;\stackrel{f_V}{\to}&amp;
                      Y(V)
                      }
              %p The fact that the two ways of going from the bottom left to the top right of the square are supposed to be equal is called. “the square commutes”, or “the square is commutative”.
          %p
            So that then is our definition of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            between generalized spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto X(V)
            are the probe-assignments that define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is defined by probe-transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_U : X(U) \to Y(U)
            such that for all maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : U \to V
            the above squares commute.
          %ul
            %li
              Such a collection of data is called a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              between the probe-assigning
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(-)
          %p
            With this understanding of maps between generalized spaces in hand, we should go back and compare it to the notion of
            %em probes
            of generalized spaces by ordinary spaces.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                we had started the discussion with observing that every ordinary topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                defines a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                of probes by the assignment
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
                \:= {maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                to
                = succeed "}" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Maps
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Maps(U,X)
            %li
              %p this way every ordinary space is a generalized space, of course;
            %li
              %p
                but that should make us nervous: because it means that now there are two
                %em different
                definitions of what a map from a test topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                to a topological space should be:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    on the one hand, it should be an element in
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mi Top
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo ,
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U) = Top(U,X)
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Top
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
                    indicates a set of maps of topological spaces;
                %li
                  %p
                    on the other hand, we can regard both
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                    and
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    as generalized spaces and then look at the set
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Sh
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(U,X)
                    of maps of generalized spaces between them, as just discussed above;
                %li
                  %p if these two notions of maps don’t coincide, then the whole picture of generalized spaces so far runs into inconsistencies!
                %li
                  %p
                    luckily it doesn’t, as one can check. Both notions of maps, while defined differently, happen to be perfectly equivalent. This statement is a simple exercise to prove, and still is the fundamental fact which makes the important idea of generalized spaces work – this fact is called the
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mi Top
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi U
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi X
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mtd
                                  %mo =
                                  %mo :
                                  %mi X
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi U
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo =
                                  %mi Sh
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi U
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi X
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          \\begin{aligned}
                          Top(U,X) &amp;=: X(U)
                          \\\ &amp; = Sh(U,X)
                          \\end{aligned}
                  %p or more formally
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Hom
                            %mi Sheaves
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ,
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo .
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          Hom_{Sheaves}(U,X) \simeq X(U)
                          \.
            %li
              %p
                as a slogan:
                %strong The Yoneda lemma says that the idea of generalized spaces determined by their collections of probes is consistent.
          %p This point is so important that it is worthwhile to build it into our very notation, so that we can’t help but think of the situation in this way.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                We agree for any two generalized spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                to write
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(Y)
                for the collection of maps from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                to
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mo =
                        %mi Sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(Y) := Sh(Y,X)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo =
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = U
                happens to be an ordinary space, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                consistently and equivalently denotes both: the collection of probes of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by the ordinary space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                into
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                regarded as a generalized space itself.
            %li
              %p
                (This notation is for instance used in the book
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
          %h2#and_more_a_topos_of_generalized_spaces And more: a topos of generalized spaces
          %p
            It turns out that we can keep going this way. There is not just a natural notion of maps between generalized spaces given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %h2#higher_sheaves_stacks
            Higher sheaves:
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Once we are at this point, we should go even a bit further: we have been talking about
            %em sets
            of probes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a true
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
            (using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
            on spaces of continuous maps). So we should be looking at sheaves with values in topological spaces: functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-) : S^{op} \to Top
            satisfying some suitable condition.
          %p
            There is only one thing we need to take care of, then: since a topological space is a bit more flexible than a plain set, we should be a bit careful about when two sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            with values in topological spaces are to be regarded as equal for all practical purposes: recall that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
            is to be thought of as the space of all ways of mapping a probe space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            into a space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                All “ways of hanging together” in a topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                are measured by a collection of groups called the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(X(U))
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                So if we are being careful, we should recognize that two sheaves
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X : S^{op} \to Top
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y : S^{op} \to Top
                must characterize essentially the same generalized space if there is a rule
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                for turning for each test space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                into probes of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi U
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_U : X(U) \to Y(U)
                %em sufficiently small
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                this comparison map of probe spaces does not change the homotopy groups, i.e. so that all induced homomorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(f_U) : \pi_n(X(U), x) \to \pi_n(Y(U), f_U(x))
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_U
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            %li
              %p
                If a comparison map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                exists with these properties (for small enough
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                defined by these sheaves are
                %em weakly equivalent
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
          %p There are several ways to deal with this complication that sheaves with values in topological spaces are very flexible, so flexible that it may require a bit of effort to determine if two of them determine the same generalized space, for all practical purposes.
          %p
            One nice way to deal with it is to again go back to our attitude that everything should be determined by how we map other things into it: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            is a weak equivalence of sheaves, as above, then we would expect that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            any other sheaf, the induced map of spaces of probes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(f) : Z(Y) \to Z(X)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
          %ul
            %li
              Recall from the discussion in the above section “maps of generalized spaces” that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(Y)
              is just another way saying “space of maps of generalized spaces from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              to
              = succeed "\u201D," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(X)
          %p
            Now, that may fail to be true, simply because we are using an awkward realization of the sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %em straighten out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            a bit, without really changing it, for the above to be true. Indeed, if things are set up correctly, there should always be a sheaf of topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z'
            which is equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z'(f) : Z'(Y) \to Z'(X)
            between spaces of probes of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z'
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a homotopy equivalence.
          %p
            The sheaves with this niceness property are called
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} infinity-stack
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-sheaf"} (infinity,1)-sheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %ul
            %li
              in applications this means that before we compute the space of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,A]
              between generalized spaces, we first have to ensure that at least one of them is sufficiently
              = succeed "." do
                %em straightened out
              = succeed "." do
                %em resolution
          %h2#generalized_cohomology Generalized cohomology
          %p
            We come back to our original motivation. Recall that we were looking at cohomology groups of spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by mapping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into other spaces. We noticed that there is in general interesting information about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which cannot be gained by mapping it into an ordinary topological space. Starting from this we have now arrived at a more general notion of space, called a higher sheaf or an
            = succeed "-stack." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                So let’s state for the record that we succeeded: we will say now for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                generalized spaces,
                = succeed "-stacks," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      H(X,A) := [X,A]
              %p
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with coefficients in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                Indeed, one finds that despite their enlarged generality, these
                = succeed "-stacks" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "-stacks" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-topos"} infinity-topos
                (more precisely: an
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} (infinity,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                of topological spaces in a more general context.
          %h2#complexes_of_sheaves_abelian_stacks
            Complexes of sheaves: abelian
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Another term for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            theory thus obtained is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
            %em abelian
            part of general cohomology. So now general cohomology is sometimes called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            to distinguish it from the more traditional cohomology theory.
          %p Here is what “abelian part of general cohomology” means:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For many sheaves that appear in practice, the topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(U)
                assigned to any test space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                is not just a topological space, but happens to have the structure of an abelian group, too.
            %li
              %p
                In this case a cascade of simplifications kicks in: first of all, such topological spaces, by a special case of something called the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
                (which is now really a precise theorem) can be identified with simplicial abelian groups.
            %li
              %p
                Second, by another theorem called the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %li
              %p
                {abelian chain complexes}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                {simplicial abelian groups}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subset
                {Kan complexes}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                {nice topological spaces}
          %p
            By an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf"} abelian sheaf
            one means a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto X(U)
            which takes values in
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex"} complex
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
          %ul
            %li
              By
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              one means nothing but the above general
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} higher topos theoretic
              notion of cohomology given by the simple idea of collections of maps from one generalized space to another. Only that in the special case of abelian sheaves a wealth of particular computational techniques are used to compute these mapping spaces (such as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived categories
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functors
          %p
            There is a collection of tools available for
            %em computing
            explicitly the cohomologies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X,A) := [X,A]
            for the case that the coefficient space can be thought of as a complex of abelian groups, or a complex of modules, these tools go by names like
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+fractions"} calculus of fractions
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorization system
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective resolution
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+and+cobar+construction"} bar and cobar construction
          %p
            and so forth; historically many concepts in abelian sheaf theory were thought to be
            %em defined
            in terms of these constructions. From a more modern perspective all these are just tools for unraveling an independent reality. This also explains why there are so many different tools which yield the same results. Most notably in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            there are two general ways for computing this cohomology called
          %ul
            %li
              %p right derived global section functor
            %li
              %p Čech cohomology;
          %p
            these two methods differ in whether one computes using “resolutions” of either the coefficient object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (this happens in the right derived global section functor approach) or in the domain object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (this happens in Čech cohomology).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Recall that we started the search for a generalized notion of space motivated by the fact that a circle bundle on a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be encoded by a map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to some topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %em with connection
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Indeed, this is now the case. There is a famous abelian sheaf, called the second
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne complex
                which we may denote by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [P_1(-), \mathcal{B}U(1)]
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi ℬ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mn 1
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \mathcal{B}_1 U(1)
                (as explained at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
                and at
